00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:53.330
of the time, it's not functioning

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:55.530
from an interest standard standpoint,

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:56.500
what does it look like?

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:57.470
- That's not good at all.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:59.840
Being down for 10% all the time,

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:00.673
that's like,

00:09:00.673 --> 00:09:02.100
no, you can't do that.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.890
- So, let's look at the
machine in question here.

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:07.540
It's this guy, the C602.

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:09.600
It's made by a company called Taylor.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:12.360
This machine is made
specifically for McDonald's.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:15.029
Taylor is like an OG
partner at McDonald's.

00:09:15.029 --> 00:09:15.862
They've been doing business together

00:09:15.862 --> 00:09:17.510
since the very beginning of McDonald's.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:19.880
They're both based like a few miles away

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:20.970
from each other in Illinois.

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:24.470
They are old business
partners going back decades.

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:27.040
But like I said, Taylor makes
ice cream machines for a bunch

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:29.270
of other fast food restaurants
like Wendy and Chick-fil-A,

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:30.960
and those machines aren't
broken all the time.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:34.290
So it's something very specific
to McDonald's and Taylor

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:36.840
that explains the ice cream machine issue.

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:39.180
If you wanna start a McDonald's franchise,

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:40.480
you sign an agreement

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:42.107
with McDonald's corporate that says,

00:09:42.107 --> 00:09:43.260
"I'm gonna open a restaurant

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:46.510
and I'll make sure that
everything is standardized."

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:49.151
It's a part of this
agreement that says you have

00:09:49.151 --> 00:09:50.620
to use this kind of French fry

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:53.070
and you have to use one
of two different companies

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:54.910
for your fryer and your grill or whatever.

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:57.770
When this new ice cream
machine came out in 2003,

00:09:57.770 --> 00:09:59.470
McDonald's had it in their agreement

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:02.410
with all their franchises,
which is like 16,000

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:05.460
in the United States, that
they had to use this machine.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:06.293
No other machine.

00:10:06.293 --> 00:10:08.580
They could not use any
other machine on the planet,

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:13.520
They had to use the C602 made
by McDonald's and Taylor.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:14.530
It was in their agreement.

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:16.210
Usually franchisees can pick

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:17.960
between multiple different companies

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:20.170
for their gear in their store.

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:22.660
Not in this case, you had to use the C602.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:24.330
So, let's dive into this machine.

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:26.913
(hiphop music)

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:30.100
Ice cream is dairy,

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:31.720
and dairy needs to be kept

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:34.080
at a certain temperature to stay safe.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:36.890
The machine itself has all these
pipes that have dairy going

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:37.723
through it all the time.

00:10:37.723 --> 00:10:40.210
So what they do once
per day is they heat up

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:42.530
the whole machine, including
the ice cream mixture

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:45.700
to like 151 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:47.570
They leave it at that
temperature for awhile,

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:49.480
while it kills off all the bacteria,

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:53.030
and then it comes back down
to a freezing temperature

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:54.470
where it turns into ice cream.

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:56.120
That process takes about four hours.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.180
And this is the thing that
you hear about all the time

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:01.500
as the explanation for why
the machine is always down.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:04.083
(gentle music)

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.460
But here's the nuance here
that really illuminates

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:10.293
the story.

00:11:10.293 --> 00:11:13.600
Usually, the employees do this overnight,

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:15.200
while the store is closed.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.450
Someone in the night shift
gets the cleaning cycle going,

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:19.070
and then they lock up for the night.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:20.070
The next morning,

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:22.980
new employees who are opening
the store for the morning,

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:25.700
show up, and they look
at the ice cream machine.

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:28.867
And on the screen, they
usually see one of two things.

00:11:28.867 --> 00:11:30.170
"Heat cycle was successful."

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:31.770
The ice cream machine is ready to use.

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:32.657
Or,

00:11:32.657 --> 00:11:34.070
"Heat cycle failed."

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:37.410
They don't know exactly why the
heat cycle failed overnight,

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:39.270
they're looking at this
interface that looks

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:41.230
like it's from like 1983,

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:42.737
and all they see is something like,

00:11:42.737 --> 00:11:44.010
"Heat mode failure,"

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:45.840
or some other obscure code

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:47.670
that doesn't really
tell you what happened.

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:49.590
The machine is completely locked out

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:52.320
until you successfully run
one of these heat cycles.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:53.740
You have to run heat cycle.

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:55.510
Even if the heat cycle missed one

00:11:55.510 --> 00:11:57.910
of these benchmarks by like one degree,

00:11:57.910 --> 00:12:00.380
it's still just gonna
tell you the thing failed.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:02.130
Doesn't tell you why or by how much.

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:03.930
So the employee tells their manager,

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:06.710
the manager has no idea
why the heat cycle failed,

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:08.047
and so they just tell
their employee to like,

00:12:08.047 --> 00:12:08.980
"Run it again."

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:10.000
Luckily it's the morning,

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.720
so, people aren't getting ice cream yet,

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:13.080
but like run the heat cycle again.

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:16.080
So, employee runs the heat
cycle again and it fails again,

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:18.390
probably for the same reason
it failed in the first place,

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:19.700
which nobody knows at this point.

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:23.600
PS, as a C602 ice cream machine insider,

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:26.190
the reason why it failed
probably is because the hopper is

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:28.110
a little bit too full of the ice cream mix

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.160
and it takes a while for
it to heat up and cool down

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:31.130
that massive matter.

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:33.930
So, just fill it a little
bit less next time.

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:36.007
That's one of many
reasons why it might fail.

00:12:36.007 --> 00:12:38.050
Anyway, nobody knows this,

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:39.350
because they're just sitting there trying

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:40.450
to serve customers.

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:41.850
I worked in fast food in high school,

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:43.860
it's a chaotic world.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:45.260
You're just trying to make people happy.

00:12:45.260 --> 00:12:48.480
Meanwhile, the person's trying
to like run the heat cycle,

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:49.450
it fails again.

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:51.290
So the heat cycle fails again.

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:52.460
Manager's pissed,

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:54.990
you have angry people asking for ice cream

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:55.823
and everyone's just saying like,

00:12:55.823 --> 00:12:57.460
"Yeah, the ice cream machines down.

00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:59.140
Sorry, we can't do anything about it."

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:01.700
So, finally, the manager
calls the franchise owner,

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:03.437
the owner of the business and says,

00:13:03.437 --> 00:13:04.350
"What do we do?"

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:05.517
And the franchise owner says,

00:13:05.517 --> 00:13:07.670
"We need to get ice cream sales going.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:08.660
This is not okay.

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:09.700
We're losing money.

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:11.429
Just call the guy."

00:13:11.429 --> 00:13:14.310
(suspenseful music)

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:16.000
Just call the guy.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:17.030
That's verbatim,

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:19.350
what one of the franchise owners told me.

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:20.183
He said,

00:13:20.183 --> 00:13:21.016
"When things are crazy

00:13:21.016 --> 00:13:23.030
I just tell them to just call the guy."

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:24.710
Now we're getting to the heart of this.

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:29.230
The guy in this case
means one guy or woman,

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:32.820
which is an authorized service
technician from Taylor,

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:34.510
the company who makes the machine.

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:35.561
Remember this?

00:13:35.561 --> 00:13:36.420
- [Male Attendant] The ice cream machine

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:39.093
isn't working right now.

00:13:39.093 --> 00:13:41.926
(dramatic music)

00:13:45.361 --> 00:13:46.985
- No, I'm just hear here to
fix the ice cream machine.

00:13:46.985 --> 00:13:47.818
- Okay.

00:13:47.818 --> 00:13:48.770
- You're from Taylor?

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:49.697
- Yes.

00:13:49.697 --> 00:13:51.040
- Okay, 'cause I've
never seen with a uniform

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:51.990
that why I'm a little bit--

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:52.823
- Okay. Yeah.

00:13:52.823 --> 00:13:53.656
I always switch things up.

00:13:53.656 --> 00:13:55.870
- Oh. So you're still Taylor?

00:13:55.870 --> 00:13:56.778
- Yes ma'am.

00:13:56.778 --> 00:13:57.611
- Okay.

00:13:57.611 --> 00:13:58.444
- Turns out that Taylor

00:13:58.444 --> 00:14:01.800
is the only company that
is authorized to repair

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:03.630
and maintain these machines.

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:04.900
It's in their contract.

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:06.470
If you call anyone else, but Taylor,

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:07.750
first off they wouldn't know how to use it

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:10.320
'cause it's such a
strange esoteric machine,

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:13.350
but also you would void your
contract, your warranty.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:15.050
So you call the service technician,

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:17.230
he or she comes to fix the machine.

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:19.800
We got our hands on some actual invoices

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:23.760
that show how much these
Taylor repair people cost.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:26.450
It's different in every
market and for every scenario

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:31.080
but somewhere to the tune of
$144 for the first 30 minutes.

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.010
And then up to hundreds of dollars

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:35.853
for each additional 15 minutes thereafter.

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:38.380
So the desperate franchise owner,

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:42.617
the one who runs the
business has to decide,

00:14:42.617 --> 00:14:46.200
"Do I deal with angry
customers who are yelling at me

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:49.330
'cause they don't have ice
cream, or do I just call the guy

00:14:49.330 --> 00:14:51.980
and get them to come
repair this at any cost?

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:52.813
It doesn't matter."

00:14:52.813 --> 00:14:53.720
So these franchise owners

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:55.900
end up paying thousands
and thousands of dollars,

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:58.090
not because their employees are competent,

00:14:58.090 --> 00:14:59.680
or they're bad at training,

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:02.150
but because this machine locks them out

00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:04.180
and they have no idea why.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:06.490
They're looking at a screen
that looks like this,

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:08.587
and all roads point to,

00:15:08.587 --> 00:15:09.970
"Call the service technician."

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:12.740
I watched a bunch of
advertisements for Taylor company,

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:14.220
people who make this ice cream machine

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:15.290
and do all the maintenance on it,

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:17.200
they hardly talk about their product here.

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:19.680
Their advertisements are more about

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:22.130
how great their repairs team is.

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:24.520
- [Advertiser] Innovators
don't build machines.

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:26.020
They solve problems.

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:28.270
This is an expert technician.

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:32.040
There are 6,500 Taylor
technicians worldwide

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:33.253
ready to help you.

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:35.481
The Taylor company!

00:15:35.481 --> 00:15:37.070
(upbeat music)

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:40.340
- A few years ago, Taylor
was in talks to be acquired

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:41.760
by another company called Middleby.

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:43.920
They put together a pitch
deck of all their financials

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:44.753
to sort of say, like,

00:15:44.753 --> 00:15:46.730
"Look at how good our business is."

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:47.870
And on that pitch deck,

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:51.130
they stated that 25%
of their revenue comes

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:54.890
from this repair and parts
service of their business.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:56.750
Yes, they make ice cream machines,

00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:58.610
but a quarter of their business comes

00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:00.400
from just maintenance and repairs.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:02.130
This sort of reoccurring revenue,

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:04.600
revenue you can rely on month after month,

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:06.500
is like gold for investors.

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:08.090
People love this because this is like,

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:11.100
you can project how much money
you're gonna get every month,

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:13.800
if you can rely on a
certain channel of revenue

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:14.633
in your business.

00:16:14.633 --> 00:16:18.670
Taylor can rely on services and repairs

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:20.410
from McDonald's ice cream machines.

00:16:20.410 --> 00:16:23.130
Remember, up until just
a couple of years ago,

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:24.760
for years and years,

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.100
McDonald's franchises were only allowed

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:28.660
to use Taylor machines.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:29.620
They had no options.

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:30.453
They couldn't be like,

00:16:30.453 --> 00:16:31.286
"Wow, this machine sucks.

00:16:31.286 --> 00:16:32.120
I'm gonna go somewhere else."

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:34.180
So they had to use the machines,

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:35.013
regardless,

00:16:35.013 --> 00:16:37.200
and they had to use this specific machine

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:39.244
that breaks all of the time.

00:16:39.244 --> 00:16:40.870
- [Taylor Advertiser] Their
parts and service network,

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:42.520
is what makes Taylor the company

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:45.820
of choice for the food service industry.

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:50.721
- No matter what time of the (beep) day

00:16:50.721 --> 00:16:53.690
the ice machine is broke.

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:54.570
How?

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:56.360
- [Mom] McDonald's is making my baby cry.

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:57.193
- How?

00:16:57.193 --> 00:16:58.930
- We got our hands on
the software for some

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:00.220
of these machines,

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:02.330
to see how Taylor has tried

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:04.790
to improve the software component.

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:05.750
You would imagine that

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:08.950
with a machine that is
notoriously broken all the time,

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:13.010
it would update their software
to give more useful feedback

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:13.843
to the user like,

00:17:13.843 --> 00:17:16.710
"Hey your cleaning cycle failed last night

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:17.930
only by one degree,

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:21.060
and that's probably because
your hopper was too full.

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:23.060
Try to fill it a little
bit less next time."

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:24.460
Alas. No.

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:26.840
Since 2003, when this thing came out

00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:29.510
there have been quite
a few software updates.

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:32.350
But instead of improving
usability of the software,

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:35.500
the software has actually
introduced just new sets

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:37.630
of cryptic error codes

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:40.260
that don't help anyone solve any problems.

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:43.670
I have spent hours in
this instruction manual

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:46.440
going through all of the error
codes and what they mean.

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:49.090
I feel like maybe just maybe,

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:51.610
after reading this multiple
times and like being

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:54.330
in the weeds, I could
operate one of these machines

00:17:54.330 --> 00:17:58.330
and maybe understand what's
going on when things fail.

00:17:58.330 --> 00:18:01.900
But it is clear to me that
this machine is not set up

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:04.890
for the user to have success in diagnosing

00:18:04.890 --> 00:18:05.960
and fixing the problem.

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:07.670
One person I was talking to was like,

00:18:07.670 --> 00:18:10.240
they had just opened a store and they,

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:11.970
it was a brand new store,
a brand new machine.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:12.803
And they're like,

00:18:12.803 --> 00:18:13.800
"Yeah, in the first few months

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.095
we called the guy like
four or five times."

00:18:16.095 --> 00:18:16.928
(chuckling)

00:18:16.928 --> 00:18:18.890
I am blown away that a product like this,

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:21.890
with software this cryptic and horrible,

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:23.830
can exist in 2021.

00:18:23.830 --> 00:18:26.160
The menus are horribly complicated.

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:28.160
The error codes are not helpful.

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:29.870
The buttons don't have words on them,

00:18:29.870 --> 00:18:31.580
instead they're just
like a bunch of symbols.

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:33.160
Like it's like press the flavor button,

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:34.750
press the ice cream button.

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:36.760
To get to here, like to access this menu,

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:38.120
press these two buttons at the same time.

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:38.953
It's like,

00:18:38.953 --> 00:18:40.940
"How could you ever expect somebody,

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:42.740
especially like an entry-level employee,

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:44.670
who's been on the job for like a few days

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:46.430
to understand any of this."

00:18:46.430 --> 00:18:48.610
It is a product that is set up to fail

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:50.860
and that has no incentive to improve,

00:18:50.860 --> 00:18:52.330
because what this means for Taylor

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:56.270
is simply more services and
repairs for their pocket.

00:18:56.270 --> 00:18:59.910
25% of their entire
business is based on this.

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:02.179
And then there's the secret menu.

00:19:02.179 --> 00:19:05.346
(bright hiphop music)

00:19:09.770 --> 00:19:12.650
In addition to the instruction
manual that's given to users,

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:13.640
I also got a hold

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:15.960
of the service manual that is given

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:18.450
to the Taylor service people;

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:20.320
like this is like the insider manual

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:22.150
that gives you the whole
picture on this machine.

00:19:22.150 --> 00:19:23.350
I do not recommend you read it.

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:24.183
In this manual,

00:19:24.183 --> 00:19:27.420
you learn about a separate menu
for the service technicians.

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:28.760
It's called the Service Menu.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:30.610
This one is not accessible to the user;

00:19:30.610 --> 00:19:32.550
the person who owns the
machine, the franchise owner.

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:33.520
It's only accessible

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:37.320
to the service techs who have
very specific code for it.

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:39.760
The existence of this
service menu is found nowhere

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:40.720
in the user manual;

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:43.250
the thing given to the
person who owns the machine.

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:47.797
And yet, according to this
manual, this menu contains quote,

00:19:47.797 --> 00:19:50.740
"Critical operating
parameters for the machine."

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:52.870
So wait, if this machine is breaking all

00:19:52.870 --> 00:19:54.760
of the time and you have a franchise owner

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:56.760
who's trying to keep
their customers happy,

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:58.857
and there's a menu where there are quote,

00:19:58.857 --> 00:20:00.920
"Critical operating parameters,"

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:02.980
that the owner doesn't have access to,

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:04.540
just call the guy.

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:06.050
Just call the guy.

00:20:06.050 --> 00:20:10.690
The machine is not meant to
give the user a clear picture

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:11.920
on why it's breaking.

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:15.210
A few weeks ago, a Taylor
service technician realized

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:18.210
that he was required to install
this new software update

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:19.650
on all McDonald's machines.

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:21.880
He noticed that the update had a bunch

00:20:21.880 --> 00:20:24.970
of weird new error codes
that made the machine worse

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:26.090
and less functional.

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:27.200
So he posted a video about it

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:29.540
asking for help from
other service technicians.

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:31.860
Shortly after he posted the video,

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:33.600
the video disappeared.

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:36.847
When asked what happened,
he responded with quote,

00:20:36.847 --> 00:20:38.690
"The system defeated me."

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:40.470
He was clearly told to
take the video down.

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:43.760
Somebody doesn't want this
sort of thing on the internet.

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:45.650
Now listen, this isn't some conspiracy.

00:20:45.650 --> 00:20:47.320
There's all sorts of weird conspiracies

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:48.630
around the ice cream machines,

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:49.570
this isn't one of those.

00:20:49.570 --> 00:20:53.040
This is actually very rational
human and business behavior.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:56.120
When one has a monopoly
over ice cream makers

00:20:56.120 --> 00:20:58.760
and they've created a
particularly (beep) product

00:20:58.760 --> 00:20:59.960
that breaks all the time

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:03.470
and gives users no clear
feedback as to why it's broken,

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:06.650
this company has no incentive to improve.

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:07.810
In an open market,

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:09.690
the incentive to make your product better

00:21:09.690 --> 00:21:10.980
comes from competition

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:13.100
and comes from basically ramifications.

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:14.220
You'll be driven out of business

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:15.520
if you don't make a good product.

00:21:15.520 --> 00:21:17.660
In this case, that's not the dynamic.

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:20.530
And in fact, having a bad
product is incentivized

00:21:20.530 --> 00:21:23.240
because it means more money
in services and repairs

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:24.110
for this business.

00:21:24.110 --> 00:21:26.280
And the reason why McDonald's
corporate is okay with that

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:28.770
is because the cost isn't born by them.

00:21:28.770 --> 00:21:31.890
It's born by local franchise
owners who have to pay

00:21:31.890 --> 00:21:33.380
for services and repairs.

00:21:33.380 --> 00:21:35.360
Taylor has the capacity to make

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:36.890
and improve ice cream makers.

00:21:36.890 --> 00:21:38.180
They do that all the time at Wendy's,

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:39.480
and Chick-fil-A, and In-N-Out.

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:43.640
And yet the machine made for
their OG exclusive partner,

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:46.910
McDonald's, doesn't receive
the same improvement

00:21:46.910 --> 00:21:47.743
and refinement.

00:21:47.743 --> 00:21:49.480
Instead it receives a bunch

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:53.490
of new error codes and
terrible user experience,

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:55.630
which always leads to "Call the guy."

00:21:55.630 --> 00:21:57.317
One franchise owner I talked to said,

00:21:57.317 --> 00:22:00.170
"All roads lead to, call the technician."

00:22:00.170 --> 00:22:02.430
Like this product is designed to get you

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:03.710
to call the technician.

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:06.620
At this moment, as I'm talking to you, 15%

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:09.190
of all ice cream machines
in the US are down.

00:22:09.190 --> 00:22:11.700
The standard for most industrial products

00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:14.610
is that you have less
than a 1% failure rate

00:22:14.610 --> 00:22:15.443
for your product.

00:22:15.443 --> 00:22:16.910
In fact, the real target

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:21.910
for mass industrial products is 99.99966%

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:24.890
of the time your product is working.

00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:29.890
That means 3.14 errors for
every 1 million opportunities.

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:35.300
What you're seeing here with
15% is a dismal failure.

00:22:35.490 --> 00:22:36.940
And this is the product of

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:39.370
no pressure or no incentive to improve.

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:43.230
And in fact, a perverse
incentive to keep it (beep)

00:22:43.230 --> 00:22:45.110
The last chapter of this story

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:48.200
is the one that is still developing today.

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:49.700
And it has to do with the email

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:51.820
I got a few months ago from somebody

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:53.700
who's deep in the middle of this.

00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:55.600
I'm gonna lay out some facts here.

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:58.820
I'm gonna let the news play out
over coming weeks and months

00:22:58.820 --> 00:23:00.150
to see how this happens,

00:23:00.150 --> 00:23:01.330
but here's what's going on.

00:23:01.330 --> 00:23:02.830
The guy who reached out to me originally

00:23:02.830 --> 00:23:05.580
was someone named Jeremy,
who is a tech entrepreneur.

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:08.370
He invented a product that
could basically fix this problem

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:10.060
of like terrible feedback

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:11.810
to the user of these ice cream machines.

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:13.440
It's this device that you plug

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:14.970
into your ice cream maker and it connects

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:17.970
with your phone and it
gives you solid reporting

00:23:17.970 --> 00:23:20.710
on all the things happening
in your ice cream machine.

00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:22.480
Something that this does not do.

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:25.450
And it identifies like actionable things

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:27.900
that you can do, to train your employees

00:23:27.900 --> 00:23:30.240
to make sure that the
problem doesn't happen again.

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:32.430
Basically, it avoids this
happening in the first place

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:34.940
and avoid you needing to
'call the guy' all the time.

00:23:34.940 --> 00:23:36.430
As soon as Jeremy launched this product

00:23:36.430 --> 00:23:38.490
it was a smashing hit
with franchise owners.

00:23:38.490 --> 00:23:39.920
They started selling hundreds

00:23:39.920 --> 00:23:42.630
of these things to franchises
all across the country.

00:23:42.630 --> 00:23:43.780
- [Reporter] People Magazine,

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:46.250
reports the chain has
partnered with Kytch,

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:48.490
a company that created
technology to help employees

00:23:48.490 --> 00:23:51.070
manage the machines and reduce downtime.

00:23:51.070 --> 00:23:52.610
- The franchise owners I talked to said

00:23:52.610 --> 00:23:55.080
that instead of getting
these vague error codes,

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:57.680
now they actually get like solid reporting

00:23:57.680 --> 00:23:59.810
on what is happening inside this machine,

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:02.690
and they can like train their
employees to help avoid this.

00:24:02.690 --> 00:24:04.150
Now lemme just caveat for a second.

00:24:04.150 --> 00:24:06.550
Jeremy and his company Kytch,

00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:09.780
obviously have a big
financial stake in this story.

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:12.320
I'm aware that he reached
out to me and gave me some

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:14.520
of his data and that
there's a big conflict

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:16.830
of interest in terms of
like neutral journalism

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:19.290
and my informant being somebody who stands

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:20.470
to gain a lot of money,

00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:21.760
if this story comes to life.

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:24.700
And that's why this story
has taken me literally months

00:24:24.700 --> 00:24:25.533
to report.

00:24:25.533 --> 00:24:28.060
I've had to go through
and independently report

00:24:28.060 --> 00:24:31.630
and verify every single thing
that Jeremy has told me.

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:33.735
Hence this.

00:24:33.735 --> 00:24:34.568
(groaning sounds)

00:24:34.568 --> 00:24:37.850
So, yes, while there is a major
stake for Jeremy and Kytch

00:24:37.850 --> 00:24:40.870
for this story and for
exactly what I'm saying here,

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:43.410
the fact is everything that I've said here

00:24:43.410 --> 00:24:46.050
checks out on my own
independent reporting.

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:47.200
Just let that be known.

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:50.400
Okay. So Kytch got so popular
among franchise owners

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:53.100
that at this conference where
all the franchise owners

00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:55.510
got together late last
year to have a conference

00:24:55.510 --> 00:24:57.280
to talk about McDonald's things,

00:24:57.280 --> 00:24:59.710
sounds like a riveting conference,

00:24:59.710 --> 00:25:01.180
the head of this organization

00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:03.460
of franchise owners gets up and says,

00:25:03.460 --> 00:25:06.360
- Now this is no silver
bullet, but it's my belief

00:25:06.360 --> 00:25:09.270
that the information it
provides increases awareness

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:11.480
and results in faster reaction times

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:12.823
when a problem does occur.

00:25:13.820 --> 00:25:15.500
Now, to be clear,

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:18.310
this is not a McDonald's
approved piece of equipment.

00:25:18.310 --> 00:25:19.850
- I mean, he sort of like endorsed it.

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:20.882
He was like,

00:25:20.882 --> 00:25:21.715
"This is a sweet product.

00:25:21.715 --> 00:25:22.840
It's actually helping us out."

00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:27.120
Soon after this endorsement,
McDonald's sends an email

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:31.700
to all of its franchises that
says that they've determined

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:35.050
that Kytch is actually super dangerous.

00:25:35.050 --> 00:25:38.140
It may cause serious safety
risks, and they recommend

00:25:38.140 --> 00:25:41.000
that the franchise owners stop using it,

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:44.020
saying that they will void
their warranty if they do.

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:44.853
Wait, what?

00:25:44.853 --> 00:25:45.700
So you have this device

00:25:45.700 --> 00:25:47.830
that is clearly solving a major problem,

00:25:47.830 --> 00:25:48.697
and instead of being like,

00:25:48.697 --> 00:25:50.000
"Whoa, tell us more about this.

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:51.420
Let's see if we can get this like approved

00:25:51.420 --> 00:25:53.740
and put it into more shops,"

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:55.060
they blacklist it.

00:25:55.060 --> 00:25:57.080
And they scare away a
bunch of their franchisees

00:25:57.080 --> 00:25:57.913
from using it.

00:25:57.913 --> 00:25:58.746
I reached out

00:25:58.746 --> 00:26:00.847
to McDonald's directly
to ask them point blank,

00:26:00.847 --> 00:26:02.150
"Like WTF?

00:26:02.150 --> 00:26:03.770
Why are you blacklisting
a product that has

00:26:03.770 --> 00:26:05.480
the potential to solve this problem

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:08.210
that you get made fun of all
the time on the internet for?"

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:11.350
They came back to me with
some boiler plate PR language

00:26:11.350 --> 00:26:14.230
about being committed to their
customers and all this stuff,

00:26:14.230 --> 00:26:16.870
being able to buy sweet
treats and all this.

00:26:16.870 --> 00:26:19.090
And then this little nugget
at the end where they say

00:26:19.090 --> 00:26:23.270
that McDonald's is testing
a connectivity solution

00:26:23.270 --> 00:26:26.563
with an approved McDonald's supplier.

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:30.540
In other words, McDonald's is working

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:34.090
on a product that does
exactly the same thing.

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:35.920
Oh, and the company that they're using

00:26:35.920 --> 00:26:39.670
to do this is called
Powerhouse Dynamics, who is,

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:44.090
wait for it, owned by the
same company that owns Taylor.

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:46.100
They're under the same parent company,

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:48.250
Taylor and Powerhouse Dynamics.

00:26:48.250 --> 00:26:50.010
They're keeping it in the family.

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:53.470
Oh, and this device, the new
one that they're testing out,

00:26:53.470 --> 00:26:56.380
doesn't give the full picture to the user.

00:26:56.380 --> 00:26:59.570
It continues to block them
out of certain aspects

00:26:59.570 --> 00:27:01.450
of the machine that could be useful

00:27:01.450 --> 00:27:03.700
in diagnosing why the
machine is always broken.

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:05.490
The theme here is the same.

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:06.520
It has been the whole time.

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:09.620
Two old companies that
have been working together

00:27:09.620 --> 00:27:10.690
for a long time,

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:14.780
looking out for each other
and keeping control over data

00:27:14.780 --> 00:27:16.560
so that they can save their own necks

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:18.610
from being disrupted by new tech.

00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:22.690
That is like a story, like
that is as old as time,

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:24.290
and it is happening right here

00:27:24.290 --> 00:27:27.160
in a potentially collusionary way.

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:28.870
Again, is this a deep conspiracy?

00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:31.080
Is there like secret
backdoor deals going on?

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:33.380
No, this is actually horribly predictable.

00:27:33.380 --> 00:27:37.160
What this is, is old
companies who are afraid

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:40.560
of being disrupted by
smarter, better products,

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:43.100
doing whatever they can to hold on

00:27:43.100 --> 00:27:44.380
to their share of the market.

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:47.110
This new connectivity product
that McDonald's mentioned

00:27:47.110 --> 00:27:49.230
to me, basically does
the same thing as Kytch.

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:52.580
But this new device doesn't
actually give the full picture.

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:55.750
It still blocks the user out
of those things that have been

00:27:55.750 --> 00:27:58.270
behind the technician's secret menu.

00:27:58.270 --> 00:28:01.110
The things that the user manual
itself says are critical.

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:04.190
It's the same old story
of keeping tight control

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:06.770
over what data and
experience the user has,

00:28:06.770 --> 00:28:09.260
so that they can continue to maintain

00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:11.410
their exclusive relationship

00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:15.430
with services and repairs,
which is 25% of their business.

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:19.260
It's honestly smart, shrewd,
cutthroat, business behavior.

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:20.900
But you know who it hurts?

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:22.300
It hurts the franchisees

00:28:22.300 --> 00:28:24.810
and it hurts all of us who
wanna go buy McFlurries.

00:28:24.810 --> 00:28:26.720
This is anti-competitive behavior.

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:28.020
Trying to squash new products

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:30.430
in the name of retaining control.

00:28:30.430 --> 00:28:31.830
So, there's a lot of nuance here

00:28:31.830 --> 00:28:34.420
that I've sort of blasted
through very quickly,

00:28:34.420 --> 00:28:38.080
but the upshot is that
McDonald's ice cream makers

00:28:38.080 --> 00:28:41.710
are broken more than everyone
else's ice cream makers,

00:28:41.710 --> 00:28:43.230
even though they're made
by the same company,

00:28:43.230 --> 00:28:45.210
because the company that makes them

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:47.280
has no incentive to improve,

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:51.000
and in fact has an
incentive to keep them bad.

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:52.350
Over the years,

00:28:52.350 --> 00:28:55.270
they've done a terrible job
improving their software,

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:58.630
and they've kept employees,
managers, and franchisees

00:28:58.630 --> 00:29:00.880
in the dark of what's actually going on

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:02.290
with their ice cream maker,

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:03.560
preventing them from actually being able

00:29:03.560 --> 00:29:04.810
to fix it themselves.

00:29:04.810 --> 00:29:06.230
And when finally someone comes

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:08.680
with a product that could actually fix it,

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:11.500
they squash it in the
name of their own product

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:13.940
that does sort of a worse version

00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:16.030
of what that new product was going to do.

00:29:16.030 --> 00:29:19.030
Kytch is suing McDonald's
for a bunch of stuff.

00:29:19.030 --> 00:29:20.840
Go read into the lawsuit if you'd like.

00:29:20.840 --> 00:29:23.730
And certainly in coming months,
we will learn the details

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:25.760
of exactly how this is all going down.

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:26.850
But for now next time you're

00:29:26.850 --> 00:29:28.940
at McDonald's and they
don't have ice cream,

00:29:28.940 --> 00:29:31.810
just know that it's not
because of lazy employees,

00:29:31.810 --> 00:29:33.990
it's not because of lack of training,

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:37.020
it's because there's an old relationship

00:29:37.020 --> 00:29:41.533
between two old companies that
don't want things to change.

